ning to initialize  uh your network as well  which we won't cover right now  um  another  thing that people do is called over  parameterization so here this  corresponds to adding more hidden units  than you kind of really need  this corresponds to having more a lot of  rows of this uh of this matrix  and the idea here is that  the more  hidden units you have the more kind of  quote-unquote chances you have  of  having the network learn something  reasonable by your data so some of the  units might die off and not be very  useful but  you know maybe like some fraction of  them will actually be useful  and the final  thing that people do is using adaptive  step sizes which is generally an  extension of stochastic gradient descent  remember as the caster grain is said you  have  we had a single step size ada which  controlled how fast you move  with methods like atograd or atom you  actually get a uh per features uh or per  uh parameter step size so for every  weight you get a number which dictates  how fast you should be moving in that  direction  and this generally leads to better  results  okay so one maybe high level thing to  keep in mind is  don't let your gradients vanish or  explode so  um if i explain this it will become kind  of clear so when you run gradient  descent or gradients the cascading  descent  if your gradients vanish which means  become  too small or close to zero then you  won't make you'll get stuck and you  won't make progress  but your gradients become too large  then you'll just explode and you will  oscillate and  might diverge  so  with careful initialization and setting  up the step sizes generally uh and even  designing of the neural uh network  architecture  all of this is around kind of making  sure that your gradients don't vanish or  explode  okay so that's all the guidance i'll  provide you there's a lot more to be  said on this topic we're just kind of  giving you a  high level overview  okay so let's summarize now so  the most imp